;;;omg

# tools/video/aac.omg
#
# An AAC audio decoder, for the sound track of an MP4 file.
#
#     import "aac.omg" as aac
#     aac.set_config(esds_bytes)           # the esds box of the track
#     alloc pcm := aac.decode(sample)      # one MP4 sample -> 1024 moments of sound
#     alloc about := aac.info()            # rate, channels, ...
#
# What it decodes: the Low Complexity profile (AAC-LC), which is what
# MP4 files nearly always carry: any sampling rate, long and short
# blocks, both window shapes, mid/side and intensity stereo, noise
# substitution, temporal noise shaping and pulses. One, two or more
# channels go in; one or two come out (more than two are mixed down).
#
# decode() gives 16-bit numbers, -32768 to 32767, 1024 for each channel
# that comes out; with two channels left and right alternate.
#
# Not decoded: the Main, SSR and LTP profiles (refused by name), and the
# extra layer of HE-AAC ("spectral band replication"): such a file
# plays, at half its sampling rate and without the top half of its
# frequencies. info().sbr says when the file declares that layer.
#
# The arithmetic is floating point, but only + - * / and sqrt are used,
# which give the same bits everywhere; the sines the tables need are
# worked out here for the same reason. So the output is the same on
# every back end, and a test can keep a checksum of it.
#
# The tables below are those of the standard, ISO/IEC 14496-3.

# The code for a change of scale factor: its length in bits, and its bits.
